.\" Manual page for the nabla command
.TH NABLA 1 "November 2026" "nabla 1.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nabla \- find the longest common subsequence of two files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nabla
[\fIoptions\fR] [--]
.I file1 file2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nabla
computes the Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) of
.I file1
and
.IR file2 .
By default, it prints their differences as an edit script; with
.B -C
it prints the common (matching) records instead, and with
.B -l
it prints only the LCS length.
.PP
Each difference is introduced by a header line:
.RS
.nf
\fB<<<<<\fR n \fIfile1\fR [\fIbegin\fR:\fIend\fR] \fB<<<<<\fR
        (records present only in \fIfile1\fR)
\fB>>>>>\fR n \fIfile2\fR [\fIbegin\fR:\fIend\fR] \fB>>>>>\fR
        (records present only in \fIfile2\fR)
.fi
.RE
where
.I n
is the one-based difference number and
.I [begin:end]
the half-open range of records involved.  A final line reports the LCS
length, and when compiled with duration reporting enabled, a timing line
such as
\fIRickSpace: 28 operations in 9 \[u00B5]s @3.111 MHz\fR
follows.
.PP
The two files are compared at one of three granularities:
.TP
.B record
each input line is one record (the default);
.TP
.B text
.RB ( -T )
the whole file is one sequence of Unicode code points, so multi-byte
characters are never split;
.TP
.B word
.RB ( -w )
each whitespace-separated word is one record.
.PP
The
.BR -A
option specifies one of four LCS algorithms, or automatic dispatch, as follows:
.TP
.B a
Automatic (the default): heuristic dispatch that selects the algorithm from
the input's edit distance, match count, and alphabet size.
.TP
.B d
Myers (1986): the O(ND) minimal edit-script algorithm
.RB ( N
the sum of the two input lengths,
.I D
the edit distance); fastest on near-identical input.
.TP
.B m
Hunt-Szymanski (1977): a threshold array over the match
lists, O((r+n) log n) time.
.TP
.B s
RickSpace (Rick 2000): forward/backward contours plus Hirschberg recursion,
O(ns) space.
.TP
.B t
RickTime (Rick 1995): a single diagonal sweep, O(ns + d) space.
.PP
Automatic dispatch selects Myers when the edit distance is small,
Hunt-Szymanski when the matches are sparse, RickTime when its match
recording fits in memory, and RickSpace otherwise.
.SH VARIANTS
.TP
.B nabla
The default 32-bit position variant.  It handles inputs of up to 4GB, which
includes the human genome (~3.1 billion), and stores each position in 4 bytes
for maximal cache locality.
.TP
.B nabla64
The 64-bit position variant.  Use it when the length of one or both inputs
exceeds nabla's 32-bit (4GB) limit.
.PP
Both variants accept the same options and produce identical results for inputs
that fit the 32-bit range.  Building from source, the default
.B make -C cli
produces
.BR nabla ,
and
.B make -C cli LENGTH_SIZE=64 BUILD_DIR=build64
produces
.BR nabla64 .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -a
Skip Affix Trimming.  By default, nabla first strips affixes (the prefixes and suffixes)
common to the inputs, runs the LCS algorithm on what remains in between, then restores
the affixes to reconstruct the result.  The
.B -a
switch can be used to infer the performance benefit of Affix Trimming.
.TP
.BI -A " a|d|m|s|t"
Specify the LCS algorithm:
.B a
Automatic (the default),
.B d
Myers (1986),
.B m
Hunt-Szymanski (1977),
.B s
RickSpace (2000), or
.B t
RickTime (1995).  Accepts
.B -Ad
or
.BR "-A d" .
.TP
.B -b
Ignore whitespace: collapse each run of whitespace to a single space before
comparing, and expand the reported spans back to the original positions.
.TP
.B -C
Print the correspondence (the common, matching records) instead of the
differences.
.TP
.B -F
Treat the input files as FASTA and strip header lines; implies
.BR -T .
Only one FASTA record of each file is used (either the first, or the record
selected by the
.I record_id
specified via
.BR -q
for the query file, or via
.BR -r
for the reference file).  Any other FASTA records will be ignored.  When no
.I record_id
is given for a file, each ignored record of that file is reported with a
warning.
.TP
.B -G
Emit the (gapped) CIGAR alignment string; file1 is the reference and
file2 the query.  Incompatible with
.BR -C ,
.BR -u ,
and
.BR -y .
.TP
.B -h
Print a usage synopsis and exit.
.TP
.B -H
Print host information (name, locale, processor, and memory) and exit.
.TP
.B -i
Perform a case-insensitive comparison.
.TP
.BI -j " join"
Coalesce nearby differences that lie within
.I join
records of each other.  In unified
.B -u
mode it also merges intervals within that distance.
.TP
.B -l
Report only the LCS length.  Skip reconstruction and formatting of the differences.
.TP
.BI -p " prefix"
Print
.I prefix
lines of context before each difference.
.TP
.B -P
Draw a progress bar to standard error while the LCS is computed.  The bar is
shown only when standard error is a terminal.
.TP
.BI -q " record_id"
Select which FASTA record of
.I file2
(the query) is used; implies
.BR -F .
A
.I record_id
names a FASTA record, as described below; without
.BR -q ,
the first record of file2 will be used by default.
.TP
.B -Q
Treat
.I file1
and
.I file2
as literal strings rather than filenames; implies
.BR -T .
Incompatible with
.BR -F ,
.BR -q ,
and
.BR -r .
.TP
.BI -r " record_id"
Select which FASTA record of
.I file1
(the reference) is used; implies
.BR -F .
A
.I record_id
names a FASTA record, as described below; without
.BR -r ,
the first record of file1 will be used by default.
.PP
A FASTA
.I record_id
identifies a record in one of two ways: If it is a numeric string, it is
interpreted as a 1-based record index.  Otherwise it will be interpreted as a
record name.
.PP
Record names may be preceded by an equal sign
.B (=)
to indicate that the subsequent string is to be interpreted as a record name,
even if it may appear to be a numeric string.  So a record whose name is all
digits is written
.BR \-q " =42"
or
.BR \-r " =42" ,
distinct from the 42nd record,
.BR \-q " 42"
or
.BR \-r " 42" .
.PP
A valid record name must be an exact, case-sensitive match on
the identifier, the first whitespace-delimited token of the '>' definition
line (defline).  A
.I record_id
that matches no record is an error.  Under
.BR -v ,
the filename, name, and description of those records actually processed are
listed to standard error.
.TP
.BI -s " suffix"
Print
.I suffix
lines of context after each difference.
.TP
.BI -t " tabsize"
Set the tab size used to expand tab characters in the parallel
.BR -y
output (default 8).
.TP
.B -T
Compare the whole file as a sequence of Unicode code points (the text path)
rather than line-by-line records.
.TP
.B -u
Emit a GNU unified diff patch of the two files (the Edit format) instead of
the default differences view.  Context lines come from
.B -p
(prefix) and
.B -s
(suffix), defaulting to 3 for whichever is not given explicitly.
.B -u
is incompatible with
.BR -C ,
.BR -F ,
.BR -G ,
.BR -l ,
.BR -Q ,
.BR -T ,
and
.BR -y .
.BR -w
with
.B -u
is not yet implemented.
.TP
.B -v
Enable verbose output: print the LCS length, the start and finish wall-clock
times, and a timing summary; by default, only the differences are shown.  With
.BR -F ,
the name and description of processed FASTA records are reported.
.TP
.B -V
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B -w
Split each line into whitespace-separated words and compare word by word
(record mode).
.TP
.BI -W " width"
Set the per-side content width used by the parallel
.BR -y
format (default 80, minimum 7).  Content is truncated to this width, so the
total output line is 1 + 2 x width characters.
.TP
.B -y
Emit side-by-side (parallel) output, in the manner of
.BR diff (1).
.PP
The
.BR -h ,
.BR -H ,
and
.B -V
switches are early-exit switches: each prints its report and terminates
without comparing files.
.PP
A
.B --
argument ends the options; the remaining arguments are operands, so a file
whose name begins with a hyphen can follow it.
.SH ALGORITHM
The
.B -A
option chooses among four algorithms, each fastest in its own regime.
Each is preceded by an Affix Trimming first pass: the maximal common prefix
and suffix (the affixes) are forced members of every LCS, so they are
stripped before the core algorithm runs on the remaining middle, then
restored to the result.  This is the first pass
.BR diff (1)
applies; it matters most for Myers, whose O(ND) work is proportional to the
sum of the input lengths.  The
.B -a
(affix) option skips it and passes the affixes through instead.
Three independent properties of the input decide which regime applies:
.TP
Edit distance
.IR D :
insertions plus deletions of the minimal edit script.
.TP
Match count \[*r]
.IR r :
the number of matched position pairs; low for sparse matches, high for
skewed or small alphabets.
.TP
Alphabet size
.IR s :
number of distinct symbols, which sizes the Rick table builds.
.PP
The algorithms and their regimes:
.TP
.B d
Myers (edit distance): fastest on near-identical input (small
.IR D ).
The O(ND) minimal edit-script algorithm
.RB ( N
the sum of the two input lengths); its reconstruction history grows
with the square of
.IR D ,
so it is bounded by a memory-derived budget.
.TP
.B m
Hunt-Szymanski (matches): fastest when the matches are sparse (low
\[*r], large alphabet), the regime its threshold array is built for.
.TP
.B s
RickSpace (space): the linear-memory fallback; O(ns) space via Hirschberg
recursion, so it never exhausts memory and completes when no other
algorithm does.
.TP
.B t
RickTime (time): fastest when the matches are dense (high \[*r]), when its
dominant-match recording fits in memory.
.PP
Automatic dispatch (
.BR -Aa ,
the default) estimates
.I D
and
.I r
from the symbol counts and selects in order:
.RS
.IP 1. 4
Myers when the edit distance lower bound is at most 2000;
.IP 2. 4
Hunt-Szymanski when the match count is at most 100 times the shorter
input length (sparse matches);
.IP 3. 4
RickTime when its recording budget covers the shorter input;
.IP 4. 4
RickSpace otherwise.
.RE
.PP
.B -v
reports which algorithm automatic dispatch selected and why.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (including
.BR -h ,
.BR -V ,
and
.BR -H ).
.TP
.B 1
Error: a command-line parse failure, a missing file, or an algorithm failure.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The locale (in particular
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.BR LC_NUMERIC )
controls the thousands separator used in the reported counts and the locale
name shown by
.BR -H .
On a system whose locale defines digit grouping, counts are printed with
comma separators (for example
.BR "LCS Length = 4,591,960" );
otherwise they are printed ungrouped.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Compare two files line by line and show the differences:
.nf
.RS
nabla old.txt new.txt
.RE
.fi
.TP
Compare side by side (parallel output) with 40-character sides and 2-space tab stops:
.nf
.RS
nabla -yp -W 40 -t 2 old.txt new.txt
.RE
.fi
.TP
Compare as text (code points), case-insensitively, showing only the length:
.nf
.RS
nabla -i -l -T a.fa b.fa
.RE
.fi
.TP
Compare a FASTA genome with one of its recorded variants (record named "100"),
using the linear-space algorithm and showing the matches:
.nf
.RS
nabla -As -C -F -q =100 ecoli_mg1655.seq ecoli_mg1655_variants.fna
.RE
.fi
.TP
Compare two records of the variants file, the "100" reference vs the "1000" query:
.nf
.RS
nabla -As -C -F -r =100 -q =1000 ecoli_mg1655_variants.fna ecoli_mg1655_variants.fna
.RE
.fi
.TP
Compare two literal strings rather than files:
.nf
.RS
nabla -Q "the quick sly fox jumped" "over the lazy brown dog"
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR diff (1),
.BR cmp (1)
.PP
The LCS algorithms and their performance bounds are described in
.I "LCS Algorithms.md"
and
.I "LCS Performance.md"
in the project documentation.
